<!--{"title": "Case study: an EU medtech start-up in an AI regulatory sandbox", "description": "How an EU medtech start-up can use the new AI regulatory sandbox rules for a hospital early-warning model: a clear test plan, real-world evidence and no false comfort.", "short": "EU AI regulatory sandbox for medtech", "h1": "Testing with the regulator in the room: an EU medtech start-up's hospital early-warning model in an AI regulatory sandbox", "kicker": "Case study · Health AI and regulation", "lede": "A European medtech start-up had built a model that warns hospital ward staff when a patient is likely to deteriorate. It worked on historical data. The questions that mattered next were harder: would it hold up on live wards, how should nurses act on it, and what would regulators expect as evidence? When the EU set common rules for AI regulatory sandboxes, the start-up decided to answer those questions in one, with a supervisor watching, without mistaking the sandbox for approval.", "date": "2026-11-15", "datetime": "2026-11-15T15:50:00+04:00", "readtime": "9 min read", "sector": "Medtech · European Union", "role": "Fractional CTO and AI architect", "engagement": "Sandbox strategy, evidence design and platform architecture", "scope": "Sandbox application, test plan, evaluation design, silent-mode deployment, clinician workflow, monitoring, documentation and exit planning", "stack": ["Hospital data feeds via FHIR", "Feature pipeline with versioned definitions", "Model registry and versioned releases", "Silent-mode scoring service", "Clinician alert interface", "Monitoring for drift and calibration", "Evidence repository", "Technical documentation set"], "outcomes": ["A sandbox plan with specific questions, success criteria and exit conditions", "Evidence from live wards gathered first in silent mode, then with clinicians", "Performance tracked by site and patient group, not only overall", "Documentation built during the sandbox rather than reconstructed afterwards", "A clear understanding that sandbox exit reports support, but do not replace, conformity assessment"], "tags": ["healthcare", "ai", "governance"], "keywords": ["EU AI regulatory sandbox", "Implementing Regulation 2026/2487", "AI Act Article 58", "medtech AI Europe", "clinical early warning model", "high-risk AI evidence", "silent mode deployment", "model calibration monitoring", "real-world evidence AI", "fractional CTO Europe"], "faq": [["What is an EU AI regulatory sandbox?", "A controlled environment set up by national authorities under the EU AI Act where companies can develop and test AI systems under regulatory supervision. Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) 2026/2487 sets common rules for how sandboxes are established and run."], ["Does taking part in a sandbox mean an AI system is compliant?", "No. Commentary on the rules notes that participation produces documentation and an exit report, but not compliance clearance or a conformity assessment. Companies still have to meet the AI Act and, for medical devices, the medical device rules."], ["Why run a clinical AI model in silent mode first?", "Silent mode scores real patients without showing results to clinicians, so the team can measure performance on live data and fix problems before anyone acts on the model's output."]], "related": ["/fractional-cto-europe/", "/healthcare-data-architecture/", "/ai-architect/"], "citations": [{"title": "Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) 2026/2487", "url": "https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg_impl/2026/2487/oj", "publisher": "EUR-Lex", "date": "11 Nov 2026"}, {"title": "Commission lays down common rules for AI regulatory sandboxes", "url": "https://eulawlive.com/commission-lays-down-common-rules-for-ai-regulatory-sandboxes/", "publisher": "EU Law Live", "date": "Nov 2026"}, {"title": "Sandboxes Without Safe Harbor: EU AI Sandbox Rules and Security Testing", "url": "https://labs.cloudsecurityalliance.org/research/csa-research-note-eu-ai-regulatory-sandbox-rules-20261112-cs/", "publisher": "Cloud Security Alliance", "date": "12 Nov 2026"}, {"title": "EU AI Regulatory Sandbox Rules Start December 1: Tests Earn No Compliance Clearance, Liability Stays", "url": "https://www.techtimes.com/articles/330540/20261112/eu-ai-regulatory-sandbox-rules-start-december-1-tests-earn-no-compliance-clearance-liability-stays.htm", "publisher": "Tech Times", "date": "12 Nov 2026"}, {"title": "The Book of OHDSI: Evidence Quality", "url": "https://ohdsi.github.io/TheBookOfOhdsi/EvidenceQuality.html", "publisher": "OHDSI", "date": "2021"}]}-->
<h2>The situation</h2>
<p>A European medtech start-up had developed a model that estimates the risk that a patient on a general hospital ward will deteriorate in the next day, using vital signs, laboratory results and nursing observations. On historical data from two partner hospitals it performed well. The founders knew that was the easy part. Clinical AI often fails in the move from historical data to live wards: data arrives late or differently, staff respond to alerts in ways nobody predicted, and performance varies between sites and patient groups.</p>
<p>As software intended to support clinical decisions, the product was likely to be regulated as a medical device and treated as high-risk under the EU AI Act. The team needed evidence that would satisfy both regimes, and had no experience of what supervisors would expect.</p>
<p><strong>Why now.</strong> On 11 November 2026, the Commission published Implementing Regulation (EU) 2026/2487, setting common rules for establishing and running AI regulatory sandboxes under the AI Act<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. Commentary was quick to point out the limits: sandbox participation produces documentation and an exit report, not compliance clearance, and liability stays with the provider<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. That suited the start-up: it wanted structured supervision and early feedback, not a shortcut.</p>

<h2>What had to be true</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Clear questions</strong>: the sandbox plan states exactly what will be tested and what success looks like.</li>
<li><strong>Patient safety first</strong>: no clinician acts on the model until it has been validated on live data.</li>
<li><strong>Evidence that travels</strong>: results usable later for the AI Act and medical device assessments.</li>
<li><strong>Performance by group</strong>: results reported by site, ward type and patient group.</li>
<li><strong>Reproducible</strong>: any result can be regenerated from versioned data, features and model.</li>
<li><strong>No false comfort</strong>: everyone understands what the sandbox does and does not provide.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Architecture</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Hospitals</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>EHR and observations<em>FHIR feeds</em></span><span>Laboratory results</span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Data</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Feature pipeline<em>versioned definitions</em></span><span>Data quality checks<em>late and missing values</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Model</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Model registry<em>versioned releases</em></span><span class="hl">Scoring service<em>silent mode or live</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Clinical use</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Alert interface<em>reason and recommended review</em></span><span>Clinician feedback</span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Evidence</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Monitoring<em>discrimination, calibration, drift</em></span><span>Evidence repository<em>for supervisors and assessment</em></span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>The same pipeline runs in silent and live mode, so evidence from one phase carries into the next.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Hospital data arrived through FHIR feeds into a feature pipeline where every feature had a versioned definition, so a result could always be traced to the exact data and code that produced it. The scoring service ran in two modes: silent, where scores were stored but not shown, and live, where alerts reached ward staff. See <a href="/healthcare-data-architecture/">healthcare data architecture</a>.</p>

<h2>The data flow, end to end</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<ol class="dgm-flow g3">
<li><b>Ingest</b><span>Observations and results arrive from the hospital in near real time</span></li>
<li><b>Check</b><span>Data quality rules flag late, missing or implausible values</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Score</b><span>The model estimates deterioration risk for each patient</span></li>
<li><b>Alert</b><span>In live mode, high-risk patients prompt a structured clinical review</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Compare</b><span>Scores are compared with actual outcomes, by site and patient group</span></li>
<li><b>Report</b><span>Monthly evidence packs go to the supervisor and clinical leads</span></li>
</ol>
<figcaption>Outcomes, not alerts, are the measure: did flagged patients actually deteriorate, and did reviews help?</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>The hard part: evidence a supervisor can trust</h2>
<p><strong>Designing the sandbox plan.</strong> The start-up's first draft asked to "test the model in hospitals". The final plan, agreed with the supervising authority, asked specific questions: does performance on live data match historical results at each site; is the model well calibrated for older patients and patients with chronic conditions; do nurses act on alerts within the expected time; and does alert volume stay manageable on busy wards. Each question had a success criterion and an agreed response if it failed.</p>
<p><strong>Making results reproducible and generalisable.</strong> Supervisors and later assessors would ask whether results could be repeated and whether they would hold elsewhere. The team borrowed the vocabulary of the observational research community: a result should be repeatable on the same data, reproducible by someone else, and generalisable to different data<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. Versioned data and features handled the first two; adding a third hospital partway through tested the last.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/eu-medtech-ai-regulatory-sandbox/evidence-attributes.webp" alt="Table of desired evidence attributes: repeatable, reproducible, replicable, generalisable, robust and calibrated, showing which of question, researcher, data and analysis stay identical or change and the expected result" width="1600" height="556" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>Attributes of reliable evidence: the sandbox plan aimed for results that were repeatable, reproducible and generalisable across hospitals. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://ohdsi.github.io/TheBookOfOhdsi/EvidenceQuality.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Book of OHDSI</a> · CC0 1.0</span></figcaption></figure>
<p><strong>Silent mode before live mode.</strong> For the first phase the model scored real patients but showed nothing to clinicians. That exposed data timing problems at one site that would have made live alerts arrive too late to help, and a calibration gap in one patient group that was fixed before any nurse saw a score.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>A sandbox is valuable for what you learn with the supervisor watching, not for the badge. Go in with sharp questions, and come out with evidence you would have needed anyway.</p></div>

<h2>How it ran</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-lanes">
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Start-up</small><ol><li>Sandbox plan</li><li class="hl">Pipeline and monitoring</li><li>Evidence packs</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Hospitals</small><ol><li>Data access and governance</li><li class="hl">Clinical review process</li><li>Nurse feedback</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Supervising authority</small><ol><li>Agree questions</li><li>Review progress</li><li class="hl">Exit report</li></ol></div>
</div>
<figcaption>The supervisor shaped the questions; hospitals shaped how alerts were used; the start-up produced the evidence.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-phases">
<div><small>Phase 1</small><b>Plan</b><ul><li>Specific questions</li><li>Success criteria</li><li>Exit conditions</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 2</small><b>Silent</b><ul><li>Live scoring, no alerts</li><li>Data timing fixes</li><li>Calibration by group</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 3</small><b>Live</b><ul><li>Alerts on selected wards</li><li>Structured reviews</li><li>Alert volume tracking</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 4</small><b>Exit</b><ul><li>Evidence pack</li><li>Exit report</li><li>Assessment plan</li></ul></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Silent mode came first and did the most to protect patients.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>Key decisions and trade-offs</h2>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Alternative</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Enter a sandbox with specific questions</td><td>Treat it as a general pilot</td><td>Clear questions make supervisor feedback useful</td></tr>
<tr><td>Silent mode before live alerts</td><td>Go live on validated history</td><td>Live data behaves differently; patients stay safe</td></tr>
<tr><td>Report by site and patient group</td><td>One overall score</td><td>Averages hide where the model fails</td></tr>
<tr><td>Versioned features and data</td><td>Ad hoc extracts</td><td>Results can be reproduced for assessors</td></tr>
<tr><td>Add a third hospital mid-sandbox</td><td>Stay with two partners</td><td>Tests whether results generalise</td></tr>
<tr><td>Plan conformity assessment in parallel</td><td>Rely on the exit report</td><td>The sandbox does not grant compliance</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>What I'd do again, and differently</h2>
<p>Again: write the sandbox questions with clinicians first, then take them to the supervisor; it made every later conversation concrete. Differently: plan alert workflow with nursing leads earlier; the hardest live-mode issues were about who responds and when, not about the model. Related: <a href="/insights/llm-evaluation-sets-that-predict-production/">evaluation that predicts production</a>, the <a href="/case-studies/health-network-fhir-omop-evidence-platform/">FHIR and OMOP evidence platform case study</a>, <a href="/insights/tech-news-2026-11-13/">the 13 November tech news</a> and the <a href="/fractional-cto-europe/">Europe page</a>.</p>
<p>If you are building clinical or other high-risk AI for Europe and want evidence that stands up, see my <a href="/ai-architect/">AI architecture work</a>, or <a href="/#assess">analyse your challenge</a> and bring the result to a call.</p>
